// Unchecked domain exception -- the common choice
public class InsufficientFundsException extends RuntimeException {
    public InsufficientFundsException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
    public InsufficientFundsException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);   // preserve the underlying cause
    }
}

// Usage -- the type name itself explains the failure
if (amount > balance) {
    throw new InsufficientFundsException(
        "withdrawal " + amount + " exceeds balance " + balance);
}
```
*Extend `Exception` instead of `RuntimeException` if you want callers to be forced to handle it.*

```java
class OrderException extends RuntimeException {
    OrderException(String message) { super(message); }   // no cause ctor!
}

try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    throw new OrderException("save failed");   // SQLException discarded
}
// Logs show OrderException only -- the real SQL error is gone.
```

```java
class OrderException extends RuntimeException {
    OrderException(String message) { super(message); }
    OrderException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);                 // chain the cause
    }
}

try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    throw new OrderException("save failed", e); // SQLException preserved
}
// Logs show: OrderException ... Caused by: java.sql.SQLException ...
```
*`getCause()` now returns the original `SQLException`, including its database error code.*

